The canonical residency violation error contract for streaming and non-streaming requests is as follows:

{
"error": {
"message": "Request blocked by data residency policy. Configure an allowed region that includes 'eu-west-1' or update your pinned region.",
"type": "invalid_request_error",
"code": "RESIDENCY_POLICY_VIOLATION",
"param": null,
"request_id": "req_...",
"help_url": "https://docs.redwoodinference.com/errors#residency_policy_violation",
"details": {
"allowed_regions": ["eu-west-1"],
"primary_region": "eu-west-1",
"attempted_region": "us-east-1",
"policy_scope": "project"
}
}
}

This error contract is enforced in the gateway to ensure that when a request is blocked by the residency policy, a deterministic, consistent error (HTTP 409) is returned instead of a generic 500 error. The enforcement is implemented in the gateway's routing service, which returns a structured policy block (409 + error_code) directly to the client. Additionally, the Python SDK would need to handle and interpret this error response according to the application's logic, ensuring that it correctly processes the standardized error payload returned by the gateway.
